Subject: fonts: text rasterisation follows the persona in every process

Patch 0117 put `gfx::FontRenderParams` in the profile and nothing read it. This
serves it: one override, in the one function every process already calls, plus
the four gates that stood between that function and Skia.

MEASURED FIRST, on the shipped macos-arm64 build, `--fingerprint=0122` with
`--fingerprint-platform=windows` and `=macos`, two launches each. The probe
draws the same three families (Helvetica, Georgia, Arial) with `fillText` at
baselines it names -- three families whose advances both personas resolve
identically on this host, so the glyph origins are identical and the only
thing left that can move a pixel is the rasterisation settings:

    metric-stable canvas, opaque   windows df91c47fa322e806
                                   macos   df91c47fa322e806
    metric-stable canvas, alpha    windows df91c47fa322e806
                                   macos   df91c47fa322e806

One digest. A composed Windows machine and a composed Mac rasterise text
byte-for-byte the same way on this host, which is to say they both rasterise
it the way THIS Mac does. That is the leak in one line, and it is the same
leak on a Linux host with the host's fontconfig in place of the Mac's
CoreText defaults.

Beside it, the second reading, and this one is an oracle rather than a
similarity:

    -webkit-font-smoothing: none   windows  on 8f0a134cb22ebc47
                                            off b8a9d3c00769b14a
                                   macos    on 8f0a134cb22ebc47
                                            off b8a9d3c00769b14a

`-webkit-font-smoothing` reaches the `SkFont` on macOS and on no other
platform: `font_platform_data_mac.mm` reads `FontDescription::FontSmoothing`,
`font_platform_data_win.cc` and the generic `font_platform_data.cc` both
declare the parameter unnamed and never look at it. So a page sets the
property, draws the same string twice and compares two canvas readbacks, and a
differing pair says "this is a Mac" whatever the user agent claims. Under a
Windows persona today the pair differs.

ONE SOURCE, AND WHY IT IS THE RIGHT ONE

`gfx::ComposedFontRenderParams()` maps the profile's tuple into
`gfx::FontRenderParams` in ui/gfx/font_render_params.cc, and the three
platform implementations of `GetFontRenderParams` defer to it:

  macos    font_render_params_mac.cc's `LoadDefaults` returns it instead of
           the constant tuple. There is nothing to blend with -- the whole of
           macOS's answer is that constant.
  linux    font_render_params_linux.cc takes it AFTER `QueryFontconfig` and
           after the postprocessing block, so `family_out` and the query cache
           keep working and only the eight rendering values change. It
           replaces the antialiasing fixup and the `device_scale_factor > 1.0f`
           rule at :267 as well, which is deliberate: subpixel positioning is
           one of the eight, and a persona whose tuple says false cannot have
           it turned back on by this host's display scale.
  windows  font_render_params_win.cc takes it inside
           `CachedFontRenderParams::GetParams`, before the
           `LegacyDisplayGlobals::SetCachedParams` call that function already
           makes, so that side effect, the cache and the `WM_SETTINGCHANGE`
           invalidation all keep working.

Overriding there rather than at each consumer is what makes the processes
agree without a second delivery path for the same eight numbers. The browser
reads it in `BrowserMainLoop::PreMainMessageLoopRun`, which feeds
`skia::LegacyDisplayGlobals` and `viz::GpuHostImpl::InitFontRenderParams`; the
GPU process receives that through `VizMainImpl`; every renderer receives it as
`blink::RendererPreferences`. **browser_main_loop.cc is therefore unchanged by
this patch, and that is the point rather than an omission** -- it already calls
`gfx::GetFontRenderParams`, so it already gets the persona's tuple, and both
of its consumers get it with it.

Two of those seats cache in a function-local static -- macOS's `LoadDefaults`
and content's `UpdateFontRendererPreferencesFromSystemSettings` -- so whether
this works at all depends on the profile existing by the first call. It does,
twice over: `InstallComposedProfile` runs from chrome_main_delegate before
content builds any child command line, and `base::apostate::Profile::Get()`
composes and installs the switch itself if it is somehow reached first. A
process that has no profile caches nullopt, which is the host's answer, which
is what an absent profile has always meant.

FOUR GATES, ALL UPSTREAM, ALL SAYING THE SAME THING

Every one of them exists because upstream is right that only Windows can
produce a contrast or gamma other than the build's own constant. Under a
composed identity that stops being true on every platform.

  1  content/public/browser/renderer_preferences_util.cc copied
     `text_contrast` and `text_gamma` under `#if BUILDFLAG(IS_WIN)`.
  2  renderer_preferences.mojom declared both fields `[EnableIf=is_win]`, so
     on a Mac or Linux build the generated accessor did not exist and
     renderer_preferences_mojom_traits.cc's `Read` was `#if`'d to match. The
     two are one change: the mojom decides whether the accessor is generated.
  3  skia/ext/legacy_display_globals.cc's `SetCachedParams` took both floats
     and then stored them under `#if BUILDFLAG(IS_WIN)`. This is the one that
     would have made the other three look like they worked: the browser, the
     GPU process and the renderer would all have passed the persona's numbers
     to a function that dropped them on the floor, and every glyph mask in the
     process would still have been gamma-corrected with this build's
     `SK_GAMMA_CONTRAST`.
  4  `WebViewImpl::UpdateFontRenderingFromRendererPrefs` is `!IS_MAC` in its
     entirety. The `WebFontRenderStyle` half of it genuinely does not apply on
     a Mac -- font_platform_data_mac.mm reads none of it -- but the
     `LegacyDisplayGlobals` half is platform-independent state that every text
     draw in the renderer reads through `SkSurfaceProps`, so under a persona
     it has to run there too.

Absent a profile none of the four moves a byte. `gfx::FontRenderParams` leaves
`text_contrast` and `text_gamma` at `SK_GAMMA_CONTRAST` and `SK_GAMMA_EXPONENT`
on every non-Windows platform, which is exactly what
`blink::RendererPreferences` and `skia::LegacyDisplayGlobals` already
initialise them to, so the newly unconditional copies are self-assignments and
the newly unconditional wire field carries the value the receiver already had.

A fifth gate is chrome/browser/renderer_preferences_util.cc, whose
`UpdateFromSystemSettings` refreshes the font preferences on Linux, ChromeOS,
Android and Windows and not on macOS. That one is gated on the profile rather
than made unconditional: calling it on a stock Mac launch would populate
`use_bitmaps`, `subpixel_rendering` and `use_subpixel_positioning` with macOS's
constants where they hold `RendererPreferences`' own defaults today. Nothing
reads them on a Mac without this patch, so it would be invisible -- and
invisible is not a reason to move bytes a stock browser does not move.

THE MACOS CORETEXT PATH

`FontPlatformData::CreateSkFont` in font_platform_data_mac.mm hard-codes
macOS's rasterisation policy, because no renderer preference reaches a Mac.
Under a persona the tuple supplies it instead, onto the settings that function
actually sets:

    antialiasing + subpixel_rendering  ->  Edging: kSubpixelAntiAlias when
                                           both, kAntiAlias when antialiasing
                                           alone, kAlias otherwise -- the same
                                           three-way choice
                                           font_platform_data_win.cc makes
                                           from use_subpixel_rendering and
                                           use_anti_alias
    subpixel_positioning               ->  setSubpixel and setLinearMetrics
    use_bitmaps                        ->  setEmbeddedBitmaps, which upstream
                                           hard-codes false
    hinting                            ->  setHinting, mapped the way
                                           RendererPreferencesToSkiaHinting
                                           maps it everywhere else (medium ->
                                           kNormal)

And the `-webkit-font-smoothing` switch is honoured only when the persona is
macOS. That is the oracle above, closed: on Windows and Linux the property
changes no glyph byte, so under a persona that is not macOS it must change
none here either. A tuple that arrives without a `ua_platform` beside it keeps
this host's behaviour rather than guessing at a foreign one.

`text-rendering: geometricPrecision` keeps its existing branch under every
persona. It only calls `setHinting(kNone)`, and hinting is inert on a
CoreText-backed typeface, so on this host it is not a second oracle. The
`setHinting` call this patch adds is inert for the same reason: it is there so
the tuple is delivered whole, and it bites only the FreeType-backed typefaces
patch 0049 gives a macOS persona off a Mac.

WHAT MOVES AND WHAT DOES NOT

Moves: the glyph coverage bytes a canvas readback returns, the same bytes
under the compositor's raster of DOM text, LCD versus grayscale coverage, the
gamma and contrast curve applied to the coverage on the platforms whose scaler
keeps it, and whether a glyph may be placed at a fractional x.

That last one reaches an advance, and it is worth being exact about because
the rest of this section says shaping does not move. `subpixel_positioning`
is an input to advance quantisation by design: `SkFont::setSubpixel(false)`
makes `SkFontGetGlyphWidthForHarfBuzz` round every glyph advance to a whole
pixel (skia_text_metrics.cc:41-45), which is exactly what a Linux desktop at
scale factor 1 reports and what `fonts.text-advances` and
coh.text-advance-subpixel-quantisation describe. So a Linux persona's
advances become integral, measured below, and that is the surface being served
rather than shaping being disturbed. Coherence holds across it because
`measureText` and a client rect come from the same `ShapeResult`: both go
integral together, which coh.measuretext-vs-clientrects requires and the probe
checks.

Does not move: anything else shaped. This patch sets `SkFont` flags and
`SkSurfaceProps`, consumed in the scaler, downstream of shaping. At equal
`subpixel_positioning` the advances are byte-identical, and the probe pins
every advance, ink box, font bounding box, client rect and layout rect against
a recording of the pre-patch binary.

Two things in this area already differ per persona and are NOT this patch.
Integer ascent and descent and glyph ink boxes follow patch 0040's Apple font
metric policy, which is coh.font-metrics-platform-rounding being served rather
than a coherence break; and a family the persona's installation does not have
resolves to a fallback with its own advances, which is the font axis.

MEASURED ON THE BUILD, AND ONE THING THE PLAN GOT RIGHT

/tmp/w2/FontRender/probe.js with page.html and baseline.json, against the
macos-arm64 build carrying this patch. 22 checks, all passing. The three
personas at seed 0122, metric-stable canvas (Helvetica, Georgia, Arial at
named baselines), two launches each:

    persona   canvas digest      advances (Helvetica/Georgia/Arial)
    windows   df91c47fa322e806   177.9296875  252.08203125  134.347412109375
    macos     df91c47fa322e806   177.9296875  252.08203125  134.347412109375
    linux     f02d5c18b74b2222   180          241           142

Read the first two rows first, because they are the interesting ones. On a Mac
host a Windows persona and a macOS persona rasterise text identically, and
identically to what the PRE-patch binary produced. That is not this patch
failing to arrive. Those two tuples differ in exactly two of their eight
fields, and neither can reach a glyph on this host:

  text_contrast  Skia's own CoreText typeface throws it away before any mask
                 is made. `SkTypeface_Mac::onFilterRec` calls
                 `rec->ignorePreBlend()` for an unhinted A8 mask unless
                 `SK_GAMMA_APPLY_TO_A8` is defined, and skia/BUILD.gn defines
                 that for Android alone (:118-125); every other branch ends in
                 `rec->setContrast(0)` with the comment "CoreGraphics dialates
                 smoothed text to provide contrast"
                 (third_party/skia/src/ports/SkTypeface_mac_ct.cpp:959-986).
                 Since Apple removed subpixel antialiasing
                 `SkCTFontGetSmoothBehavior()` is `none` here, which forces
                 hinting to kNone and the mask to A8, so the first branch is
                 the one that runs. Checked from outside this patch as well:
                 `--text-contrast` and `--text-gamma`, which content copies to
                 the renderer and which override at `GetSkSurfaceProps` time,
                 move no pixel at any value from 0 to 3.9, under GPU raster
                 and under `--disable-gpu` alike, while `--disable-gpu` itself
                 changes the digest -- so the probe is sensitive and the lever
                 is not.
  use_bitmaps    only reaches a face carrying an embedded strike, and these
                 three do not.

So Windows-on-Mac canvas text is the Mac's, which is what
docs/METHODOLOGY.md section 9 says about the surfaces a fork cannot reach: the
claimed operating system costs whatever the host will not give up, and the
compositor's job is to report that rather than pretend it away. It is now
measured instead of assumed, with the Skia line that causes it.

The third row is the proof that the chain works, and it is the reason the
linux persona is in the probe at all. Its tuple differs from the Windows one
in four fields, and `subpixel_positioning` is one CoreText does honour: the
canvas digest moves, both screenshot crops move, and the advances go integral.
Profile -> `gfx::ComposedFontRenderParams` -> `blink::RendererPreferences` ->
`FontPlatformData::CreateSkFont` -> `SkFont::setSubpixel`, end to end, on the
host that is hardest to serve.

The rest of the battery: two launches of one persona and two reads in one
launch agree for all three (determinism); advances identical across windows
and macos at the three numbers above; every metric identical to the pre-patch
recording for windows and macos; and the `-webkit-font-smoothing` oracle now
reads on == off under the Windows and Linux personas and on != off under the
macOS one, which is the one page-visible tell this patch closes on a Mac host.

RESIDUALS

The glyph masks stay the host scaler's. These are the flags a Windows Chrome
sets on its `SkFont`; the coverage bytes underneath come from CoreText on a
Mac and FreeType on Linux, and DirectWrite's are not reachable from either by
any setting. Same in reverse: a macOS persona on Windows gets DirectWrite
masks.

Of the eight fields, four are inert on a Mac host and the reasons differ.
`text_contrast` and `text_gamma` are discarded by
`SkTypeface_Mac::onFilterRec` as above. `hinting` is overridden by the same
function, which collapses every level to kNormal or kNone. `autohinter` is
stripped there too, in `flagsWeDontSupport`. All four are live on a Linux
host, where `SkTypeface_FreeType::onFilterRec` keeps the pre-blend whenever
LCD text is on (SkFontHost_FreeType.cpp:892-896) and the hinting level reaches
FreeType intact -- and a Linux host is the deployment target, which is where
the surface leaked worst to begin with.

What this means for an operator is one sentence, and docs/LIMITATIONS.md
'Text rendering follows the persona' says it: on a Mac host the rasterisation
a page can read is the Mac's for a Windows persona, on a Linux host it is the
persona's.

Verified: `scripts/checkfile.sh` clean on ui/gfx/font_render_params.cc,
ui/gfx/font_render_params_mac.cc, skia/ext/legacy_display_globals.cc,
content/public/browser/renderer_preferences_util.cc,
chrome/browser/renderer_preferences_util.cc,
third_party/blink/common/renderer_preferences/renderer_preferences_mojom_traits.cc,
third_party/blink/renderer/core/exported/web_view_impl.cc and
third_party/blink/renderer/platform/fonts/mac/font_platform_data_mac.mm. The
traits file is the mojom's proof as well: `data.text_contrast()` does not
exist on a Mac build while the field is `[EnableIf=is_win]`, so a macOS
compile of an unconditional `Read` is the EnableIf removal taking effect.
ui/gfx/font_render_params_linux.cc and ui/gfx/font_render_params_win.cc cannot
be compiled on this host and were not; both are a single guarded assignment
placed where the surrounding code's own comments say the value is finished.
The probe ran twice: against the shipped pre-patch binary, which produced the
baseline this patch quotes, and against a macos-arm64 build carrying this
patch, where all 22 checks pass. What is still unmeasured is a Linux host,
which is where four of the eight fields are live and where the leak is worst;
the same probe answers it unchanged, given a Linux build.

